    "updated_at": "2021-12-07T21:53:11Z",
    "body": "Aw jeez.\r\n\r\n[The culprit is `__wezterm_osc_7` in the shell integration](https://github.com/wez/wezterm/blob/7deb215303ce1a8e64d48f65ef3e0a1d24fc2fbc/assets/shell-integration/wezterm.sh#L387), which attempts to set the title of the terminal using a `wezterm` command.\r\n\r\nThings that stop this bug from occurring are:\r\n\r\n- Redefining `__wezterm_osc7` to only use the `printf`-based method instead of the `wezterm` based method. I don't know why this works, since both the wezterm call and the printf call *appear* to be blocking.\r\n- Adding the following to the `wezterm.lua`, which prevents this hook from being added to pre-exec:\r\n\r\n```\r\n    set_environment_variables = {\r\n        WEZTERM_SHELL_SKIP_CWD=\"true\",\r\n    },\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotably, using the `bash_preexec` framework does *not* help in this case, because earlier pre-exec jobs that get backgrounded can continue to run, affecting the reported jobs count.\r\n\r\n@jameschensmith If you don't need WezTerm's title bar to update, you can use the snippet in my second bullet point. If you do, you can try applying it anyways, then using starship's pre-prompt hooks to draw it yourself, e.g. define the following:\r\n\r\n```\r\n__update_terminal_title(){\r\n   printf \"\\033]7;file://%s%s\\033\\\\\" \"${HOSTNAME}\" \"${PWD}\"\r\n}\r\nstarship_precmd_user_func=__update_terminal_title\r\n```",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
