    "body": "> How did you install WezTerm?\r\n\r\nI pulled down the latest Debian package from the WezTerm install docs, and installed with `apt`.\r\n\r\n> When tracing my execution, I found a reference to `. /etc/profile.d/wezterm.sh` meaning that the WezTerm integration scripts were run at a system level (meaning only for login shells).\r\n\r\nThat makes sense. So, when I had WezTerm spawn a non-login shell, it must not have run the integration scripts? I'm not quite familiar with `set -x`, but when I enable it after I spawn a login shell (i.e. `bash -l` then `set -x`), I definitely see a lot more output (output specifically related to content from the WezTerm script we've been discussing).",
