    "body": "It's a little late, so this is my last post for tonight. I copied the starship init code to my `~/.bashrc` so that I could debug a bit further. By adding `jobs -l` before actually counting the jobs, I get the following output:\r\n\r\n```\r\n[1]  90794 Done                    wezterm set-working-directory 2> /dev/null\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis interestingly also resets the job number. If I run `jobs -p` instead, the number is **not** reset. The `90794` in the example above is of course the process ID which would have been counted. I validated this by running `jobs -p`, then `set -x` and comparing the process ID's outputted by both `jobs -p` executions (the one I added for debugging & the one which starship uses to count jobs).\r\n\r\nWith this all in mind, I have to assume the problem is that `wezterm` and many more commands act like z/autojump by having some spurious background jobs. `jobs -l` seems to either wait for the job to finish, or at least is reporting that the job is in `Done` status. Still not sure what the resolution to this is, though :confused: ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
